26	 * Game settings: `string` was the only field type with no allowlist
27	 *
28	 *   `validateConfig` constrains `enum` to its option list, `int` to its bounds
29	 *   and `bool` to a boolean — but `string` accepted any 256 characters,
30	 *   INCLUDING newlines. Those values are written verbatim into LINE-ORIENTED
31	 *   config files on the customer's live VM by worker/game-config.ts:
32	 *
33	 *     updateKeyValue()  ->  server.properties     `motd=<value>`
34	 *     updateConvars()   ->  Rust server.cfg       `server.hostname "<value>"`
35	 *     applyValheim()    ->  launch-args file      `-world "<value>"`
36	 *
37	 *   A newline therefore does not set one option, it appends further ones. A motd
38	 *   of "hi\nenable-rcon=true\nrcon.password=x" turns RCON on with a chosen
39	 *   password — and worker/game-config.ts then reads rcon.port back out of that
40	 *   same file and interpolates it into an `mcrcon` command line UNQUOTED
41	 *   (`-P ${shq(port)}`), so an injected value reaches a shell.
42	 *
43	 *   This is the allowlist for that field type, applied at the single point the
44	 *   UI, the server action and the worker all validate through.
45	 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
